<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">E. M. Forster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Edward Morgan Forster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">E. M. Forster was an English novelist and essayist, author of A Room with a View (1908), Howards End (1910) and A Passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> (1924). In 1921 he served as private secretary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukoji-rao-iii-of-dewas-senior">Tukoji Rao III of Dewas Senior</a>, an experience he described in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hill-of-devi">The Hill of Devi</a> (1953). A Passage to India, drawing on his Indian years, became a classic of writing about British rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/e-m-forster.jpg" alt="E. M. Forster" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dora Carrington (1893–1932) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E._M._Forster_von_Dora_Carrington,_1924-25.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward Morgan Forster was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 1 January 1879, the only child of Alice Clara Whichelo and the Welsh architect Edward Morgan Llewellyn Forster, who died of tuberculosis before his son's second birthday. From 1883 to 1893 he and his mother lived at Rooks Nest, near Stevenage, which became the model for the house in Howards End. A legacy from his great-aunt Marianne Thornton gave him enough to live on and allowed him to become a writer. At King's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, he joined the Apostles, a secret discussion group, and met Lytton Strachey and Leonard Woolf. He later belonged to the Bloomsbury Group.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After travelling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1903, Forster published his first novel, Where Angels Fear to Tread, in 1905. The Longest Journey followed in 1907, A Room with a View in 1908, Howards End in 1910 and A Passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1924. His short story The Machine Stops (1909) is often viewed as the beginning of technological dystopian fiction.</p><p>He spent six months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1912 and 1913, visiting his friend Syed Ross Masood, to whom he later dedicated A Passage to India. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he was a conscientious objector and served as a Chief Searcher for missing servicemen for the British Red Cross in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In the early 1920s he returned to India as private secretary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dewas">Dewas</a>, recounted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hill-of-devi">The Hill of Devi</a>, and on coming back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> he finished A Passage to India. In the 1930s and 1940s he was a notable broadcaster on BBC Radio.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Many of the novels examine class differences and hypocrisy, and his humanism lies at the centre of his work, which often shows the pursuit of personal connection despite the restrictions of society. Howards End is a condition-of-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> novel about the Schlegels, the Wilcoxes and the Basts, and A Passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> takes as its subject relations between East and West in the later days of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>. It won the James Tait Black Memorial Prize.</p><p>Maurice, a story of homosexual love, was completed in 1914 but published only in 1971, the year after his death on 7 June 1970. Forster was open about his homosexuality with close friends but not with the public. He declined a knighthood in 1949 and received the Order of Merit on his 90th birthday.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “E. M. Forster” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some pacifists changed their position: Bertrand Russell called his stance on Nazism relative pacifism, and <b>E. M. Forster</b> and Leonard Woolf rejected their earlier pacifism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>E. M. Forster</b> served as secretary to Maharaja Tukoji Rao III in 1921, an experience he described in The Hill of Devi.</blockquote><a href="/state/dewas-senior">Dewas Senior</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Coventry, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1938" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of E. M. Forster"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1938">What was here in 1938? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
